                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 827,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 796,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Shifts everything in an array along an axis, and what falls off one end comes back at the other.\n\nNothing is lost: it is a rotation of the order, not a shift into empty space. Use it to scroll an image or a pattern round and round, to offset one copy of a sequence against another, or to step through a circular list. If you want old values to drop off and new ones to arrive, that is a buffer, not a roll - see np.rolling_buffer.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.roll <shifts: int> <axis: int>\nDefaults: shifts 2, axis 0.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.roll 2 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Each array that arrives is rolled and sent. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\nshifts (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many places to move. Positive moves towards the end (down for axis 0, right for axis 1); negative the other way. A shift larger than the axis just goes round again: on 6 columns, 7 is the same as 1. Changing it does NOT send by itself - in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second, so the picture follows your edits. A negative shift rolls the other way: -1 moves everything one place back, the first column wrapping round to the end.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich axis to roll along: 0 moves rows, 1 moves columns. For a 1-D array only 0 means anything. A negative axis counts from the end (-1 is the last axis), and an axis past the last one is treated as the last.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrolled array:\nSame shape and values as the input, moved round along the axis.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.roll 2 1 moves every column 2 places to the right; the two columns pushed off the right edge reappear on the left, so the F's stem now sits in the middle. Step shifts up and the F scrolls right, wrapping;\nset axis to 0 and it scrolls down instead, its top bar wrapping to the bottom.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.flip reverses instead of shifting. np.rot90 turns by quarter turns. np.rolling_buffer keeps a moving window of recent values, dropping the oldest. t.roll is the PyTorch version.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 780,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Shifts everything in an array along an axis, and what falls off one end comes back at the other.\n\nNothing is lost: it is a rotation of the order, not a shift into empty space. Use it to scroll an image or a pattern round and round, to offset one copy of a sequence against another, or to step through a circular list. If you want old values to drop off and new ones to arrive, that is a buffer, not a roll - see np.rolling_buffer.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.roll <shifts: int> <axis: int>\nDefaults: shifts 2, axis 0.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.roll 2 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ninput:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Each array that arrives is rolled and sent. Only this inlet makes the node send.\n\nshifts (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many places to move. Positive moves towards the end (down for axis 0, right for axis 1); negative the other way. A shift larger than the axis just goes round again: on 6 columns, 7 is the same as 1. Changing it does NOT send by itself - in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second, so the picture follows your edits. A negative shift rolls the other way: -1 moves everything one place back, the first column wrapping round to the end.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich axis to roll along: 0 moves rows, 1 moves columns. For a 1-D array only 0 means anything. A negative axis counts from the end (-1 is the last axis), and an axis past the last one is treated as the last.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrolled array:\nSame shape and values as the input, moved round along the axis.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nnp.roll 2 1 moves every column 2 places to the right; the two columns pushed off the right edge reappear on the left, so the F's stem now sits in the middle. Step shifts up and the F scrolls right, wrapping;\nset axis to 0 and it scrolls down instead, its top bar wrapping to the bottom.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.flip reverses instead of shifting. np.rot90 turns by quarter turns. np.rolling_buffer keeps a moving window of recent values, dropping the oldest. t.roll is the PyTorch version."
                },
